The elbow and hand sit on the same tree as the shoulder: they are the upper limb, and I treat them with the same approach. Here surgery is smaller and more precise, and recovery depends heavily on rehabilitation.

# Elbow fractures

> An elbow immobilised for long becomes stiff.

Radial head, olecranon, complex articular fractures (coronal shear). Many are fixed with plates or screws to allow early movement.

# Elbow instability

> After a dislocation the ligaments may not heal well.

The same applies after a fracture-dislocation. Ligament reconstruction restores stability.

# Tennis elbow

> Treated with exercise. Surgery is for resistant cases.

Tennis elbow and golfer's elbow are overload tendinopathies. The work is on exercise and load management.

# Stiffness

> Arthrolysis restores motion lost after trauma.

Open or arthroscopic, on an elbow that has lost range of movement.

# Wrist and scaphoid

> The scaphoid heals poorly if it is not treated correctly.

The distal radius often breaks in a fall on the hand. This is why the scaphoid deserves special attention.

# Tendons and nerves

> Small procedures, often under local anaesthesia.

Carpal tunnel syndrome, trigger finger, tendon injuries. Recovery is quick.

# Microsurgery

> A tool for the cases that require it.

I trained specifically in microsurgery and reconstruction (Seattle, London). I use it in nerve injuries and complex reconstructions. It is not a catalogue service.

